of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a26c8a3-a13b-58d6-bfaf-c5d8a3cc7448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373128} {:answer/id #uuid "50967f0f-57f9-55b7-90b8-95fc4e3fa730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373129} {:answer/id #uuid "2e061036-3998-585e-b278-0208758b12d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373130} {:answer/id #uuid "557e2071-1c97-50d4-8a32-945633714865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373131} {:answer/id #uuid "b54a407b-bd86-5732-aaae-37311ea6d93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b54a407b-bd86-5732-aaae-37311ea6d93f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373132} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60df298-4f9c-5b83-a3a6-23ee39146a33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20b472e3-9e5e-5f24-9f58-3446c6fe971d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79326" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=5$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
